{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.AppendixSurreals.DiophantineBirthdayTrajectory","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.AppendixSurreals.DiophantineBirthdayTrajectory","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.NumberTheory.Transcendental.Liouville.LiouvilleWith","lean:module:Mathlib.NumberTheory.Transcendental.Liouville.LiouvilleNumber","lean:module:QOU.AppendixSurreals.DiophantineBirthdayScope","lean:module:QOU.AppendixSurreals.GonshorConvergence"],"declarations":10,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AppendixSurreals.rothExponent_implies_liouville_direction","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AppendixSurreals.rothExponent_implies_liouville_direction","kind":"theorem","line":297,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/AppendixSurreals/DiophantineBirthdayTrajectory.lean#L297-L306","doc":"⚠ The hypothesis is **not** vacuously available: `RothExponent` is a strictly stronger\nstatement than anything proved here, since it constrains *every* algebraic irrational at\n*every* exponent above `2`, while the unconditional results only ever conclude\ntranscendence from approximability.\n\nRecorded because the temptation, on finding a hypothesis one cannot discharge, is to weaken\nit until it becomes provable — at which point it stops carrying the separation. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AppendixSurreals.roth_separates","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AppendixSurreals.roth_separates","kind":"theorem","line":281,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/AppendixSurreals/DiophantineBirthdayTrajectory.lean#L281-L295","doc":"**Conditional on Roth, `LiouvilleWith 3` genuinely separates.**\n\nBoth halves are here, and only the first needs the hypothesis:\n\n* no algebraic irrational satisfies it — this is Roth;\n* some transcendental does — `liouvilleNumber 2`, unconditionally, from Mathlib.\n\nThat is what \"separated by trajectory\" has to mean to be worth anything: a criterion\ntaking different values on the two classes. Compare `no_birthday_only_separation`, where\nthe criterion provably cannot. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AppendixSurreals.RothExponent","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AppendixSurreals.RothExponent","kind":"def","line":273,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/AppendixSurreals/DiophantineBirthdayTrajectory.lean#L273-L279","doc":"**Thue–Siegel–Roth** (Roth 1955): an algebraic irrational is not approximable to any\norder beyond `2`.\n\nA known theorem, **not** formalised in Mathlib and not proved here. Anything below that\nneeds it takes it as an explicit hypothesis. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AppendixSurreals.liouvilleWith_of_frequent_runs","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AppendixSurreals.liouvilleWith_of_frequent_runs","kind":"theorem","line":222,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/AppendixSurreals/DiophantineBirthdayTrajectory.lean#L222-L249","doc":"**Infinitely many runs of length `n·t` ⟹ `LiouvilleWith t`.**\n\nThe implication the trajectory picture always asserted, now proved: run structure in the\nsign expansion controls the Diophantine exponent.\n\n`Irrational α` is required by `LiouvilleWith`'s own `x ≠ m / n` clause — a rational `α`\neventually *is* one of its dyadic approximants, and the predicate excludes that case by\ndefinition rather than by accident. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AppendixSurreals.slowApproximation_vacuous_at_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AppendixSurreals.slowApproximation_vacuous_at_one","kind":"theorem","line":192,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/AppendixSurreals/DiophantineBirthdayTrajectory.lean#L192-L207","doc":"⚠ **At `p = 1` the statement above is not merely false but VACUOUSLY TRUE**, which is\nthe trap worth having in the corpus.\n\n`liouvilleWith_one` makes *every* real approximable to order `1`, so the hypothesis\n`¬ LiouvilleWith 1 x` is never satisfied and the implication holds with no content.  A\nreader who instantiated the target at a convenient exponent would obtain a **proved Lean\ntheorem** whose statement reads like the conjecture and which says nothing — worse than\nthe false version, because it carries a proof.\n\nThis is the same failure mode as `birthday_avg_le` in `Basic.lean` (satisfied vacuously\nunder any bounded birthday) and as `DiophantineBirthday`'s **former** stipulated field\n(since replaced upstream, bean `qou-ffwk`): a statement whose form suggests content it\ndoes not carry. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AppendixSurreals.SlowApproximationImpliesAlgebraic","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AppendixSurreals.SlowApproximationImpliesAlgebraic","kind":"def","line":169,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/AppendixSurreals/DiophantineBirthdayTrajectory.lean#L169-L190","doc":"The naive converse of `liouville_trajectory_direction_settled`: that slow rational\napproximation is enough for algebraicity.\n\n⚠ **This is FALSE at every `p`, and is named here only so the conjecture's wording is not\nmistaken for it.**  Almost every real is transcendental with approximation exponent exactly\n`2`, so for any `p > 2` there are transcendentals satisfying `¬ LiouvilleWith p x` — the\nhypothesis holds and the conclusion fails.  It is deliberately **not** asserted, instanced,\nor used as a hypothesis anywhere.\n\n**Roth's theorem does not repair this direction**, and it is worth being explicit because\nthe conjecture's gloss cites Roth.  Roth sharpens the *settled* direction — algebraic\nirrationals have exponent exactly `2`, hence `LiouvilleWith p x` with `p > 2` already\nforces `x` transcendental, improving on Liouville's degree-dependent bound.  It says\nnothing about reals that approximate slowly.  So citing Roth buys a better exponent on the\ndirection that is already closed, not progress on the one that is open.  (Roth is in any\ncase not in Mathlib; Liouville is.)\n\nThe genuinely open content of `conj:diophantine-birthday` is therefore neither this nor\n§2, but the **surreal** claim: that the sign-expansion trajectory computes the\napproximation exponent.  That is the bridge §3 of the header says is unbuilt. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AppendixSurreals.liouvilleNumber_two_transcendental","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AppendixSurreals.liouvilleNumber_two_transcendental","kind":"theorem","line":154,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/AppendixSurreals/DiophantineBirthdayTrajectory.lean#L154-L157","doc":"A **concrete witness** that the settled direction is not vacuous: `liouvilleNumber 2`\nis approximable to every order, hence transcendental. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AppendixSurreals.liouville_trajectory_direction_settled","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AppendixSurreals.liouville_trajectory_direction_settled","kind":"theorem","line":143,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/AppendixSurreals/DiophantineBirthdayTrajectory.lean#L143-L152","doc":"**Liouville's theorem, as the trajectory conjecture's settled direction.**\n\nIf a real is approximable to every order — the strongest possible trajectory condition —\nit is transcendental.  Discharged from Mathlib, not assumed.\n\nThis is why labelling `conj:diophantine-birthday` as wholly open overstates it: one\ndirection of the separation is a theorem in the corpus's own toolchain. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AppendixSurreals.liouvilleWith_separates","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AppendixSurreals.liouvilleWith_separates","kind":"theorem","line":124,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/AppendixSurreals/DiophantineBirthdayTrajectory.lean#L124-L136","doc":"**And it does not collapse.**  Unlike `birthday ∘ ofReal`, which is constant on the\nirrationals and therefore separates nothing, `LiouvilleWith 2` takes both truth values:\n`liouvilleNumber 2` satisfies it and `0` does not.\n\nThis is the exact point where the trajectory version departs from the birthday version.\n`no_birthday_only_separation` says every predicate on the birthday agrees on `√2` and `π`;\nno such collapse is available here, because the criterion is already non-constant. ","formalizes":[{"@id":"block:thm:diophantine-birthday-trajectory","label":"thm:diophantine-birthday-trajectory","md":"quantum-observable-universe/appendix-surreals-archimedean/diophantine-birthday-trajectory.md"}],"signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AppendixSurreals.liouvilleWith_rat_shift_invariant","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AppendixSurreals.liouvilleWith_rat_shift_invariant","kind":"theorem","line":115,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/AppendixSurreals/DiophantineBirthdayTrajectory.lean#L115-L122","doc":"**The Liouville exponent is a function of the trajectory tail.**\n\nChanging finitely many signs of a sign expansion shifts the real by a rational, and\n`LiouvilleWith p` does not see that shift.  So this criterion is well defined on tails —\nthe precondition any trajectory criterion has to meet. ","signatureChunk":0}]}